  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q-generate-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": "Generate some other triples using Plato’s method, starting with $12,16,20,\\ldots$ .\n\nPlato’s method: start with a number divisible by $4$ as the first side length. Square it, divide by $4$ and subtract $1$ to obtain the second side. Square the original number, divide by $4$ and add $1$ to obtain the hypotenuse.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Sample answers: $(12,35,37)$, $(16,63,65)$, $(20,99,101)$.",
      "worked": "For a starting side $n$, the other side is $\\frac{n^2}{4}-1$ and the hypotenuse is $\\frac{n^2}{4}+1$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\frac{12^2}{4}-1&=36-1=35\\\\ \\frac{12^2}{4}+1&=36+1=37\\\\ \\frac{16^2}{4}-1&=64-1=63\\\\ \\frac{16^2}{4}+1&=64+1=65\\\\ \\frac{20^2}{4}-1&=100-1=99\\\\ \\frac{20^2}{4}+1&=100+1=101 \\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe triples are $(12,35,37)$, $(16,63,65)$ and $(20,99,101)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 66
  },
